eep  putting it off for another day another  day another day and they never actually  get to enjoy the fruits of what they've  done that doesn't mean you blow it it  doesn't mean that you are  celebrating excessively or you're not  putting aside money for charity or for  relatives or things like that but i  think it's  this kind of  abstemiousness can become a  self-perpetuating cycle and it's  important to celebrate the victories and  it's important to enjoy our lives along  the way  yeah now if you've ever sat with  somebody who's passed passing away they  never sit there and say i wish i worked  harder  they usually say i wish i took that  vacation  so yeah um i said we're gonna we're  gonna wrap this up um but kevin kelly is  gonna get the the last question  um  what is your 10-year project that you  are working on  so my 10-year project i alluded to this  a little bit uh during during the talk  but in 2016  i decided that i was going to learn to  write musical theater and it actually  came out of a very specific circumstance  in 2015 the year before  i  went a little crazy with work i guess  you could say i was launching a book and  i  and the honest truth was  i was  feeling a little  depressed and so  as a way of  burying that  i went on the road  and so i gave 74 talks that year  which was a lot  uh it came out to about one and a half  talks every week  and so that meant i was pretty much on a  plane  once or twice every week and i was so  incredibly exhausted at the end of the  year i was i was sick i was just run  down and i thought you know why why am i  doing this why am i why did i decide it  was a good idea to run myself to the  point of exhaustion  and i was living in new york at the time  and i realized  why am i paying so much money to live in  new york when i'm not actually in new  york i'm just on planes it seemed really  ridiculous and so i came up with a new  year's resolution for myself and my new  year's resolution  was that as a way of  leaning into the city as a way of kind  of reconnecting and appreciating where i  was  i was going to do one uniquely new york  activity per week  something that you could only do in new  york so you know maybe it's see a  broadway show or go to a museum or you  know take a  you know dumpling tour of chinatown or  you know whatever it is  so one of the first things that i did  was a guy that i knew invited me to a  broadway show and so i said yes i will  go to that show with you it was like my  first week's activity  and i went to this show i went to see  fun home which won the 2016 yes won the  2016 tony award a really amazing show  and the truth was i lived in new york  about a year and a half by that point i  had only been to one broadway show i  really hadn't taken advantage of that  but i saw this show and it just hit me  and i  went home and i woke up the next morning  and i was seized with this feeling with  this this this sense that i needed to  learn to write musical theater  and i decided to pursue that and  i have done so since then and so i i've  taken you know trainings and fellowships  and and moved it forward but what i've  come to learn is that the average show  that it's it's a long process it takes  about seven years on average seven or  eight years for a show to go from  inception to actually making it to  broadway there's a lot of steps along  the way so in 2016 i decided that i  would create a 10-year goal for myself  to have a  show on broadway for the 2026 season so  we're in 2022 so i'm working on it  thank you so much  you  [Music]  you

